# Untitled

Facile Synthesis of \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o M o O}_{4}\) Heterostructure Nanosheets for Enhanced Acetone Detection
ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations s\*ı Supporting Information
KEYWORDS: nanosheets, heterojunction, \(p\) -type metal oxides, acetone, gas sensors
Inevitably, the rapid industrialization of emerging economies around the world is contributing to the release of various toxic volatile organic compounds (VOCs) into the atmosphere, which is a major health hazard for the general population. Among the VOCs, acetone is a common byproduct of research and industrial operations,1 the prolonged exposure to which results in liver and kidney failure in humans.2 To effectively prevent accidental acetone exposure in the workplace and guarantee the safety and health of workers, therefore, we must develop reliable sensors that can provide accurate, quantitative detection capability toward acetone gases in real-time.3
Semiconducting metal oxide (SMO)-based gas sensors have been widely utilized for the detection of various target gas molecules, including VOCs, in a highly sensitive manner. Up to now, various n-type SMOs such as \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}{}^{4}\cdot\mathrm{WO}_{3}{}^{5}\) \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{}^{6}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{}^{7}\) have been widely explored for use as sensing material. Pristine p-type SMOs, on the other hand, have been rarely studied in this application owing to their inherently low response. Specifically, according to a study by Hübner et al.,8 the response of a p-type SMO-based sensor was about equal to the square root of that of an n-type SMO-based sensor with identical morphologies. Nevertheless, p-type SMOs such as \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},^{9}\mathrm{\NiO},^{10}\mathrm{\CuO},^{11}\mathrm{\Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},^{12}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{}^{13}\) have recently received a fair share of attention owing to their high catalytic reactivity and relatively low cost as chemiresistive sensing materials.9 In particular, many p-type SMOs act as catalysts for the selective oxidation of VOCs, which promotes the selective
response of the sensor toward the target VOCs.14 Given these inherent advantages of p-type SMOs toward selective gas sensing, as long as the issues with the low sensitivity of p-type SMOs are resolved through a more in-depth materials design, we should be able to achieve a practically viable sensor system based on p-type SMOs.
One representative type of a p-type SMO is \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) a catalytically active material typically used as a photocatalyst15 or an electrocatalyst,16 which makes it a suitable candidate for selective gas sensing applications.9 Specifically, considerable amounts of studies have been conducted to explore and improve the gas-sensing characteristics of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) by modifying their electronic structure. For instance, Li et al.17 synthesized \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocages for selective acetone detection, which was further sensitized with Au to improve the sensing performance. In spite of its effectiveness, noble metal catalysts increase the cost of the sensing material, and the catalyst nanoparticles are prone to aggregation in high-temperature environments. As such, noble-metal-free alternatives are more attractive for practical applications. In this regard, Li et al.18 demonstrated
the preparation of flower-like nanostructures consisting of numerous heterojunctions between n-type \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) phases and p-type \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) phases, which can amplify the gas sensing properties through the interface electron flow effect. In n-p junctions, however, the charge carriers readily undergo frequent recombination and mitigate the sensitization, which motivates the exploration of nanostructured SMO-based sensing layers based on abundant \(\mathbf{p}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{p}\) heterojunctions.20 Moreover, two-dimensional materials have been widely used in catalysts such as the hydrogen evolution reaction19 owing to their highly exposed active sites.21,22 To this end, while rarely studied in the literature thus far, \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) -based \(\mathbf{p}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{p}\) heterojunction materials should provide an important opportunity for achieving highly sensitive p-type SMO-based chemiresistive sensors.
In this study, metal−organic-framework (MOF) derived ptype \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/\mathrm{p}\) -type \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) (hereafter, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4})\) heterostructure 2D nanosheets (NSs) were prepared for the first time, and their utility toward acetone detection is demonstrated. Herein, a facile but effective synthesis strategy was introduced by using celolite imidazole framework-L (ZIFL) NS as a template. Specifically, ZIF-L acted as the sacrificial host, and the \(C\mathrm{o}^{2+}\) ions in the porous structure of ZIF-L were converted into \({\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) (in the presence of \(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) ions) for their regrowth onto the template upon hydrothermal reaction, the former of which was calcined into \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) in the subsequent step to produce heterostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs. The experimental results reveal that the acetone sensing behavior of heterostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs is superior to that of pristine \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and physically mixed (PM)- \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) composite sensors as control samples,
in terms of higher sensitivity, ultralow limit of detection (10 ppb), and superior long-term stability. This study paves a new, cost-effective pathway to improve the gas sensing performance of p-type SMO through constructing \(\mathsf{p}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{p}\) heterojunction structures.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate (Co \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) , 2- methylimidazole (2-MIM), sodium molybdate dihydrate \(\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\right.\) \(2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) , and ethanol were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. All chemicals were used as received without further purification.
Synthesis of Co-MOF NS. A total of \(8\ \mathrm{mmol}\) of 2-MIM and 1 mmol of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were dissolved in \(20\;\mathrm{\mL}\) of deionized water. Then, the two solutions were mixed in a beaker, and the resultant solution turned purple immediately. After the reaction at room temperature for \(^{5\mathrm{~\scriptsize~h,~}}\) the sample was collected by using centrifugation, and collected Co-MOF NS was washed in ethanol several times and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{5\mathrm{~h~}}\) .
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{CoMoO}_{4}\) NS. A total of \(0.25{\mathrm{~g~}}\) of Co-MOF NS synthesized above was dispersed in \(45\,\mathrm{\mL}\) of deionized water containing \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (various amounts: 0.01, 0.02, 0.03, and \(0.04\textbf{g}\) corresponding to \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (5 at. \(\%\) Mo), \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/\) \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (12 at. \(\%\) Mo), and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (16 at. \(\%\) Mo), respectively) uniformly by ultrasonication for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) to form a suspension (for clarification, the atomic ratios reported in this manuscript corresponds to the actual measured values as confirmed by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) measurements). The resultant suspension was maintained in an oven at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) . Next, the sample was collected using a vacuum filter, and collected \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@\mathrm{Mo}\) NS was washed using ethanol and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{5\,\mathrm{h}}\) . At last, the prepared \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@\mathrm{CoMo}_{4}\) NS was first calcined at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) with a
ramping rate of \(5~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\), followed by calcination at \(600~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\) with a ramping rate of \(10~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\).
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) NS, \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{MoO}_{4},\) and Physically Mixed (PM)- \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}/\mathbf{CoMoO}_{4}\). The synthesis of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NS is similar to that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NS except for the absence of the reaction with an aqueous solution of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) is prepared as follows: \(7.0\,\mathrm{\mmol}\) \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(1.0\,\mathrm{\mmol}\) \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\right)_{6}\) \(\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{34}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and \(28\ \mathrm{mmol}\) \(\mathrm{CO}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\) are dissolved in \(70~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water homogenously. Next, the solution is transferred into a \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave followed by a heat treatment at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\). Then, the sample with pink color is collected following the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NS collecting method. Physically mixed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (denoted as \(\mathrm{PM-CO}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\)) is prepared by sonicating \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol solution containing \(197~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \(19.6~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) (calculated atomic ratio of Mo: \(8.5\%\)).
Preparation of Sensors and Evaluation of Gas Sensing Performance. The fabrication of sensors is reported by our previous study.23 Air and varying concentrations of target gas were injected into the gas-sensing chamber based on \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) air and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) target gas as a complete cycle. The response of sensors was calculated as \(R_{\mathrm{gas}}/R_{\mathrm{air}},\) where \(R_{\mathrm{gas}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{air}}\) are the resistances in target gas and ambient condition, respectively.
DFT Calculations. The spin-polarized DFT \(+\ U\) \(\left(U=2.0\ \mathrm{eV}\right.\) for \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\)) calculations were performed using the plane-wave projector augmented-wave (PAW) method,25 applying the semi-local Perdew−Burke−Ernzerhof (PBE) exchange-correlation functional, as implemented in the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) code.26 More details are given in the Supporting Information.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The synthesis of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs is illustrated in Figure 1. In the first step, the Co-MOF nanosheet was homogeneously grown in an aqueous growth solution containing Co precursors \(\left(\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) and the organic linker molecules (2-methylimidazole). The as-obtained Co-MOF sheets were subsequently mixed with \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) in an aqueous solution and then subjected to a hydrothermal treatment (schematically illustrated in Figure S1). In this case, the Co-MOF is a porous structure with a large surface area and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) is evenly distributed in the porous structure of Co-MOF.27 Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) plays two roles: generating molybdate acid radicals \(\left(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}\right)\) and inducing an alkaline environment \(\left(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\right)\). Thus, the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ions reacted with either \(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}\) or
\(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions in the aqueous solution to form \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2},\) respectively, which is readily deposited onto the template. The regrowth process continues, transforming the MOF into a \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) heterostructure while maintaining the initial 2D sheet-like morphology of the ZIFL template. The \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) portion of this heterostructure could be further oxidized to \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) through calcination, ultimately yielding the heterostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analyses were carried out to investigate the morphologies and structures of the as-made samples. The SEM images in Figure 2a (Figure S2, low magnification SEM image) show that the Co-MOF NSs with a smooth surface was successfully obtained through the aqueous reaction. After the hydrothermal process, the NSs could maintain their 2D morphology but turned slightly rough with several debris on the surface (Figure 2b and Figure S3) formed by the in situ regrowth and sedimentation of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4},\) which are insoluble in water. The NSs retained their structure even after calcination, as shown in Figure 2c and Figure S4. Moreover, TEM images also support the successful synthesis of the NSs as shown in Figure 2d and Figure S5. Notably, the high-resolution scanning TEM (STEM) images in Figure \({2\bf g}\) and Figure S6,b,c revealed the porous structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs, wherein the abundant pores on NSs (Figure S6a) would promote the permeation of gas molecules into the sensing layer for effectively eliciting chemiresistive response. In addition, a homogeneous distribution of Mo on the \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) -based NS without obvious aggregation is confirmed according to the energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) element mapping results (Figure 2e,f). We further conducted a high-magnification EDS mapping as shown in Figure 2i; the area with concentrated Co signals and empty Mo signals (encircled in yellow dotted lines) is ascribed to the crystalline \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4},\) while the remaining area showing both Co and Mo signals is assumed to be \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) . Furthermore, the HRTEM image in Figure 2h presents the lattice fringes with interplanar parameters of 0.290, 0.243, and \(0.334\,\mathrm{\nm},\) corresponding to the (220) and (311) planes of cubic \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and the (002)
plane of monoclinic \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4},\) respectively. Altogether, we could confirm that nanoscale grains of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) coexist in an even mixing, together constituting the porous 2D NS structure and that this structure would facilitate highly sensitive gas sensing performances.
To investigate the crystal structure of the as-synthesized NSs, we performed powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analyses on the \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@M o\) NS, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo, real value as confirmed by ICP-MS measurements), PM\(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4},\) \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) NSs, and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (Figure 3a, and Figure S8). For reference, the XRD patterns of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) NSs and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) each showed peaks assigned to cubic \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) (JCPDS, PDF#42-1467) and monoclinic \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (JCPDS, PDF#21-0868), respectively. Importantly, the diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs as well as the \(\mathrm{PM-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) indicated the coexistence of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) crystals. As elucidated previously, \(\mathrm{PM-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NS was prepared by physically mixing crystals of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4},\) thus exhibiting stronger and sharper diffraction peaks, which indicate low dispersity and better crystallinity. On the contrary, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) \((8.5\%)\) NSs exhibit broadening in their diffraction peaks compared with the other three samples (Figure 3), indicating decreased grain sizes (TEM image in Figure S7a,b) as a result of the in situ growth strategy. The minor diffraction peaks (i.e., (204), (441), and (440)) corresponding to \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) were not strong enough to be observed with clarity. In Figure 3b, as the Mo content increased from 5.0 to 16 at. \(\%,\) the diffraction peak located at \(2\theta=36.9^{\circ}\) corresponding to the (311) plane of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) showed a noticeable broadening, which indicates that the growth of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) grains were impeded. Since the main peak located at \(2\theta\) \(=26.5^{\circ}\) corresponding to the (002) plane of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) was also becoming stronger, which was attributed to the increasing amount of crystalized \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4},\) we may conclude that the presence of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) prohibited the growth of \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) grains. Altogether, the TEM and XRD analyses confirmed that \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs having nanograins with regulated sizes could be successfully prepared through our in situ growth strategy, which would induce the formation of a large number
of p-p heterojunctions for improving the sensing performances of p-type Co-based SMOs.
To further analyze the composition and chemical states of the synthesized NSs, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)
analyses were performed (Figure 4 and Figure S9−10). The XPS spectra of the as-synthesized \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs revealed the existence of Co 2p, Mo 3d, and O 1s peaks, which confirms the mixed oxide composition. In more detail,
the Co 2p region (Figure 4a) showed two main peaks attributed to \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) (779.75 eV) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) (795.01 eV) peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\), respectively. Two smaller peaks for the \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) (781.26 eV) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) (796.69 eV) peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) were also located. These peaks confirm the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) in the as-prepared NSs, supporting the formation of spinel-structure \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). In addition, the two peaks in the Mo 3d region as shown in Figure 4b at 235.0 and \(231.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to the \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) and \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) peaks of \(\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}\) as their peak separation (3.1 eV), respectively, coinciding with that of previously reported values for \(\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}\). Furthermore, the spectrum of O 1s can be deconvoluted into three Gaussian peaks at 529.9 (lattice oxygen, denoted as \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}}\)), S31.4 (defect oxygen, denoted as \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{def}}\)), and \(532.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) (adsorbed oxygen, denoted as \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}\)). Specifically, the proportion of the combined sum of \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{def}}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}\) over the entire O 1s signal \(\left(S_{\mathrm{che}}\right)\) represented the surface chemisorbed oxygen species that can participate in the redox reaction with incoming acetone molecules, inducing a change in resistance. The \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{che}}\) in the case of heterostructure \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. % Mo) NSs (59.9%) was significantly higher than that of pristine \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (32.5%) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (27.1%), indicating that the heterostructure provides additional chemisorbed oxygen species on the surface. Based on the fact that the \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{che}}\) of \(\mathrm{PM-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) was calculated as 43.4%, we concluded that the synergistic effect of the heterojunctions facilitates the chemisorption of oxygen.
To directly evaluate the effect of p-p heterojunctions on improving the gas sensing characteristics of the p-type Co-based SMOs, we characterized the chemiresistive responses of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NSs, \(\mathrm{PM-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs (with different amounts of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\)) upon exposure to
varying concentrations of acetone as well as other interfering gases \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{8},\,\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH},\,\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\,\mathrm{CH}_{4},\,\mathrm{CO},\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},\,\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\) (Figure 5, more information of sensors are shown in Figure S11). Previous studies on SMO-based gas sensors indicated that the working temperature is a critical parameter that affects the sensing performance since the surface adsorption behavior can directly be affected. Hence, we first optimized the working temperature by exposing \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs, as the representative sample, to acetone of 5 ppm at various working temperatures (200−400 °C). As shown in Figure S12, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs exhibited the highest response to 5 ppm acetone at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\); henceforth, the evaluation of the dynamic sensing behavior (to 10 ppb−5 ppm acetone gas) was conducted at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to establish a fair comparison between each sample.
We then compared the baseline resistance of each sensor as shown in Figure 5d and found that the baseline resistance of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. % Mo) NSs (39 kΩ) is much higher than that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (4.6 kΩ), which is ascribed to several factors including the inherent poor conductivity of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\), numerous heterointerfaces that serve as charge barriers, abundant chemisorbed oxygen species, and the grain size effect. We found that the electrical resistances of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs showed a strong correlation with increased amounts of \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\), supporting our understanding. As shown in Figure 5a, the response of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. % Mo) NSs to 5 ppm acetone is shown to be 8.5, which is 7.1 times higher than that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NSs (response = 1.2). Similarly, we found that all of the sensors containing both \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\), including the case of the physically mixed sample, can achieve a higher response to acetone gas compared to
pristine \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) albeit with high baseline resistance. Specifically, the responses of \(\mathrm{PM-Co_{3}O_{4}/C o M o O_{4}}\) NSs were 2.3, the decreasing response of which is attributed to the limited amount of heterojunction interfaces for physically mixed samples due to the low dispersity of Mo-containing regions (Figure S13).
The dynamic response of the various sensing materials to 50 ppb acetone is shown in Figure S14, from which we found that all of the \(\mathrm{CoMoO_{4}}\) -containing materials (including the physically mixed sample) exhibited an improved response, while pristine \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NSs showed practically undiscernible changes in the resistance. It is noteworthy that \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\) \(\mathrm{CoMoO_{4}}\) NSs (with different contents of \(\mathrm{CoMoO_{4}}\) ) exhibited an exceptionally low limit of detection (LOD, defined as the lowest concentration of the analyte gas that can be detected) of \(10~\mathrm{ppb}\) (Figure 5c). We further investigated their selectivity toward acetone gas by exposing \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo) NSs, the representative sample, to various common interfering gases \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{8},\right.\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{CH}_{4},\) CO, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) \(\mathrm{NO}_{2})\) , each at a concentration of 5 ppm (Figure 5f, Figure S15). We found that the heterostructured NSs exhibited excellent selectivity toward acetone gas with a significantly higher response \(\left(R_{\mathrm{gas}}/R_{\mathrm{air}}\,>\,8\right)\) compared to those of other analytes \(\left(R_{\mathrm{gas}}/R_{\mathrm{air}}\,<\,2.1\right.\) for toluene, which was the secondhighest). Taken together, we demonstrated that the interface engineered p-type MOSs, such as \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) by incorporating numerous heterojunctions in the nanostructure, can effectively enhance the sensing performance.
In real-life applications, the detrimental effects of humidity on the response of the SMO-based gas sensors are often cited as a major development challenge.36 Since the electrons donated from the water vapor would recombine with
electronic holes to decrease the number of charge carriers, the baseline resistance of wet p-type SMOs would increase, as demonstrated in Figure 6b and Figure S16.37,38 Interestingly, the response of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo) NSs under a high RH of \(90\%\) (2.97 toward 1 ppm acetone) was only slightly degraded \((9.0\%)\) compared to the response in a dry atmosphere (3.30 toward 1 ppm acetone), demonstrating the resilience of NSs even in a highly humid environment. Furthermore, long-term stability and reliability are also crucial to the practical use of gas sensors. To investigate the acetone sensing stability, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo) NSs were exposed to 1 ppm acetone for 15 successive cycles, where each cycle consists of a \(10\;\mathrm{\min}\) injection of air (baseline) followed by acetone (target gas). The dynamic response to 1 ppm acetone gas is plotted as shown in Figure 6a, demonstrating stable responses of 3.4 on average (standard derivation of 0.05) throughout 15 cycles with no noticeable fluctuation and/or degradation in the signals. Even after the sensor was kept in ambient conditions for 2 months after initial fabrication, the dynamic response curves of the aged sensors (Figure 6a) were not too different from the as-synthesized sensors, implying the excellent long-term stability of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) heterojunction NSs. We further established the reliability of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo) NSs by evaluating it over an extended cyclic exposure of 82 cycles, as illustrated in Figure 6c. Moreover, the stability test results of pristine \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NS and \(\mathrm{PM-Co_{3}O_{4}/C o M o O_{4}}\) are presented in Figure S17. Altogether, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs synthesized in this study can improve the sensing response without further degradation of stability.
To elaborate on the explicit mechanistic details of the chemiresistive sensing of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) NSs, we performed
additional analyses accompanied by theoretical calculations and considerations. The origin of the chemiresistive response of typical p-type SMO-based sensing materials is the reaction of target gas molecules with chemisorbed oxygen on the sensing layers, which changes the charge carrier population on the surface to modulate the overall resistance.36,38,39 Specifically, the sensing process involves (i) the chemisorption of oxygen species on the sensing layers (eq 1) resulting in the formation of hole accumulation layers, which helps decrease the resistance of sensors; and the (ii) release of electrons back to the sensing layer and their recombination with holes upon the reaction of acetone molecules with chemisorbed oxygen (eqs 2 and 3), thus increasing the electrical resistance. To identify the energy level between the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4},\) ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements were carried out as shown in Figure S18. The work function \((W_{\mathrm{f}})\) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was calculated as \(5.17\;\mathrm{\eV}\) (5.045 eV from density functional theory (DFT) calculations), while that of the pristine \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) could not be collected owing to its poor conductivity. According to the calculated \(W_{\mathrm{f}}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\) \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) (8.5 at. \(\%\) Mo) NSs of \(5.50\;\mathrm{eV},\) it was speculated that \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) would exhibit a larger \(W_{\mathrm{f}}\) than \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) which is also supported by DFT calculations.40 It seems reasonable to deduce from these results that the formation of the heterojunction interfaces between \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4}\) domains caused the migration of electrons from \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) to \({\mathrm{CoMoO}}_{4},\) inducing the formation of the depletion layers at the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) interface. When the surface is exposed to acetone, more electrons will be released back to the sensing layers after the acetone reacts with chemisorbed oxygen species to amplify the change in resistance.
